# Implementation and assumptions

Implemented `decode_prefix`, `decode_exact`, `serialize`, and `identify` in
Rust 1.98.1 using only the standard library. `main.rs` is the entry point;
`codec.rs`, `json.rs`, and `sha256.rs` contain the helpers. `tests.rs` and an
internal codec test module contain the local tests. `candidate` is the compiled
submission executable.

The initial supplied specifications were `language.md` and `interface.md`.
They define the JSON interface but do not define the transaction wire grammar,
hashing algorithm, resource-limit semantics, or detailed error classification.
The separate repair phase supplied six bounded examples in `feedback.json`.
The following documents current behavior; choices beyond those demonstrated by
the supplied examples remain assumptions:

- Wire encoding uses little-endian fixed-width integers, canonical CompactSize
  lengths, and raw previous-transaction digest bytes without reversal.
- `version_bits`, `previous_index`, `sequence`, and `locktime` are unsigned
  32-bit quantities. `amount` is a signed 64-bit quantity encoded in eight
  little-endian two's-complement bytes, as demonstrated by repair feedback.
  No consensus,
  script-validity, monetary-range, or transaction-policy checks are performed.
- Witness mode also accepts legacy encodings. A zero input count activates
  marker/flag interpretation. Flag 1 introduces per-input witness stacks;
  unknown flag bits and a witness encoding with every stack empty are malformed.
  A stack containing one empty item is nonempty. A zero flag is treated as the
  zero output count of a legacy transaction with no inputs or outputs.
- Serialization emits witness framing only when requested and at least one
  witness stack has an item. A legacy serialization with no inputs and nonzero
  outputs is allowed, although witness-mode decoding of those bytes is
  ambiguous and may fail.
- Transaction identifiers are double SHA-256: txid hashes stripped bytes,
  wtxid hashes full bytes, and display order reverses the digest bytes.
- `decode_prefix.consumed` counts bytes from the requested offset. An offset
  beyond the input is `invalid_request`; an offset at EOF yields a truncated
  encoding. `decode_exact` rejects any trailing bytes as `malformed_encoding`.
- `max_items` limits each CompactSize-prefixed collection separately, including
  script bytes and witness-item bytes. It is not an aggregate budget. Omission
  means no additional numeric cap (`u64::MAX`). A zero limit permits empty
  collections. Serialization checks only collections included in its output;
  `identify` checks both encodings. The decoder checks whether the remaining
  bytes can hold the declared count at its minimum item size before enforcing
  the item limit. An impossible payload is `malformed_encoding`; otherwise an
  excessive count is `resource_limit`. It does not fully parse an over-limit
  collection to discover later errors. This precedence generalizes the supplied
  truncated-input counterexample.
- Missing required fields, wrong JSON types, out-of-range decimal strings, and
  invalid hex yield `invalid_request`. Integer strings accept leading zeroes
  and a leading minus sign for amounts, but not plus signs, whitespace,
  fractions, or exponents. Hex is case-insensitive
  on input and lowercase on output. Every input requires its `witness` array.
- Unknown JSON fields are ignored; duplicate fields use the last value.
  Absent or null `limits` uses defaults; an explicit null `max_items` is invalid.
  Blank lines are invalid requests. Each line is processed independently,
  including a final line without a newline.
- JSON nesting beyond 256 is `admission_limit`. Failed explicit allocation
  reservations are `resource_limit`; caught processing panics are
  `execution_failure`. No arbitrary transport-byte cap is imposed, so there
  is no configured trigger for `transport_limit`. The documents do not specify
  precedence when a request has multiple independent errors.

## Build and validation

Build command from `language.md`:

```sh
/Users/donavonguyot/RosettaBitcoin/Nodes/RosettaNode/.local/toolchain-wrappers/rustc --edition=2024 -C opt-level=2 -C linker=/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/clang main.rs -o candidate
```

Local tests:

```sh
/Users/donavonguyot/RosettaBitcoin/Nodes/RosettaNode/.local/toolchain-wrappers/rustc --edition=2024 --test -C opt-level=2 -C linker=/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/clang main.rs -o tmp/candidate_tests
./tmp/candidate_tests
```

All 25 tests passed after repair (17 initial tests and eight added tests).
Coverage includes SHA-256 known answers, a known genesis
transaction identifier, literal legacy/witness encodings, all truncations of
two fixtures, prefix offsets, integer extremes, CompactSize boundaries,
nonminimal lengths, witness edge cases, limits, JSON validation, 1,000 generated
transaction round trips, 20,000 random malformed inputs, and 10,000 mutations of
valid encodings. All six supplied feedback examples are exact-response
regressions. Additional repair tests cover signed amount bounds, rejected
out-of-range amounts, and malformed/resource precedence at each vector type.
A separate initial executable smoke test exercised all four operations and
recovery after invalid requests. No external dependencies, network access,
reference execution, or evaluator access were used; repair feedback came only
from the user-supplied `feedback.json`.

## Repair outcome

All six supplied cases were reproduced as failures before modifying the codec.
Five shared the unsigned-amount assumption: negative values were either
rejected in structured input or decoded as large positive values. Using `i64`
for amounts fixes decoding, serialization, and both identifiers while retaining
the original wire bits. The sixth case required checking that a declared
payload can exist before returning an item-limit error. Both repairs are in
`codec.rs`; all six examples now match their supplied expected responses.
